A1. Intelligent. ~ KNOWING
A6. About the lowest thing on earth. ~ REPTILE
A12. What farmers want the railroads to lower. ~ RATE
A14. Ancient agricultural chariot. ~ CART
A15. South Carolina (abr.). ~ SC
A17. Individually. ~ SEVERALLY
A20. A famous President of the U. S. (Initials). ~ TR
A21. A chip of the old block. ~ SON
A23. A kind of treatment that Christian Scientists don't believe in. ~ MEDICAL
A24. A Greek who in olden times herded goats instead of running a candy store. ~ PAN
A25. A time in March that Caesar was told to beware of. ~ IDES
A27. What Henry Clay said he would rather be than President. ~ RIGHT
A28. Something a young lawyer needs when he hangs out his shingle. ~ CASE
A29. What giraffes are long on (pl.). ~ NECKS
A31. Definite article. ~ THE
A32. Something no spendthrift does with his money. ~ BANKS
A33. What small boys look for at a party (pl.). ~ EATS
A35. Something dogs do when they're winded. ~ PANT
A36. What you should hold your best girl in (not sofa). ~ ESTEEM
A37. You get a lot of Luckys or Fatimas in one. ~ CARTON
A39. Something you would like at the Follies. ~ SEAT
A41. Two of a kind. ~ TEAM
A43. A diamond ring acts like one. ~ CHARM
A45. Sherman called it something else. ~ WAR
A47. A step or set of steps for getting over a fence or wall. ~ STILE
A49. What he becomes after the ceremony. ~ HERS
A50. What the teacher did to the bad boy. ~ CANED
A52. The last word in prayers. ~ AMEN
A53. A city in St. Louis Co., Minn. ~ ELY
A54. Whose voice does the pup hear in the victrola ad? ~ MASTERS
A56. The part of a novel most girls read first. ~ END
A57. Member of parliament (abr.). ~ MP
A58. One of Louis XV's lady friends. ~ POMPADOUR
A60. A preposition. ~ TO
A61. What Solomon was supposed to be even though he did marry so promiscuously. ~ WISE
A62. A Mexican proletarian. ~ PEON
A64. The way your wife smiles when you buy her a new hat. ~ SWEETLY
A65. Helps. ~ ASSISTS

D1. Something naughty but nice. ~ KISSING
D2. Correlative of "either." ~ OR
D3. First person singular, past tense of the verb "be." ~ WAS
D4. A sum entered in an account. ~ ITEM
D5. When will prohibition be enforced? ~ NEVER
D7. Brilliant (fr.). ~ ECLAT
D8. Something you will wear when you take a ride in that little black hack. ~ PALL
D9. What you should do if at first you don't succeed. ~ TRY
D10. Neuter pronoun of the third person singular. ~ IT
D11. Something you can't be in when you're fooling. ~ EARNEST
D13. Fear. ~ FRIGHT
D16. A system of signals. ~ CODE
D18. Something editors do (not cuss). ~ EDIT
D19. Something that even a very little tooth can do. ~ ACHE
D20. Work imposed by another. ~ TASK
D22. What liberty is to happiness. ~ NECESSARY
D24. A play in which there is no talking (and often very little acting). ~ PANTOMIME
D26. Those who sometimes get on thin ice. ~ SKATERS
D28. A poem set to music. ~ CANTATA
D30. Something all janitors hate to send to your room in the winter. ~ STEAM
D32. Uncovers. ~ BARES
D34. What the absent-minded professor doesn't do to his alarm clock. ~ SET
D35. Mike's partner in Irish humor. ~ PAT
D38. Politicians' and capitalists' stock in trade (pl.). (Not oil). ~ SCHEMES
D40. Mah Jongg's sister. ~ FANTAN
D42. Those who have no bananas (Italian or Greek). ~ VENDORS
D44. Something a drowning man cries for. ~ HELP
D45. This will sting you. ~ WASP
D46. Something that 24 horizontal used to play on. ~ REED
D48. A time of the year when you give up things you don't like any way. ~ LENT
D50. "I'll walk a mile for one," said the Arab when his Ford broke down. ~ CAMEL
D51. Aviation poison (pl.). (It doesn't take many to kill you.) ~ DROPS
D54. Superlative of "more." ~ MOST
D55. What the butcher does when he can't collect (not cries). ~ SUES
D58. An essential in all "slapstick" movie comedies. ~ PIE
D59. King (fr.). ~ ROI
D61. You and I. ~ WE
D63. Opposite points of the compass (abr.). ~ NS


